      "rev_n_1_7a80f7_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_7a80f7_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_7a80f7",
        "thought_text": "Iterative Prime Summation with Correct Handling of Input Constraints",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_7a80f7_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_3f65b776b8",
        "created_at": 1794503046.8990593
      },
      "rev_n_2_b801b4_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_b801b4_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_b801b4",
        "thought_text": "To solve the Bachgold problem correctly, we need to ensure that we always use the smallest prime numbers available to maximize the number of primes in the sum. Specifically, we should prioritize using the prime number 2 whenever possible, because it allows us to split the number into more parts. For odd numbers greater than 2, we should use the prime number 3, as it also helps in maximizing the count of primes. This approach ensures that we achieve the maximum number of primes for any given integer \\( n \\).",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_b801b4_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_01ae22ad94",
        "created_at": 1794503067.376741
      },
      "rev_n_3_d78d73_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_d78d73_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_d78d73",
        "thought_text": "Dynamic Programming Solution\n\nThe issue lies in how the `prime_factors` list is being updated. Instead of appending the prime factors directly, we need to ensure that we are adding the correct combination of prime factors that sum up to `i`. This requires a more sophisticated approach to update the `prime_factors` list during the dynamic programming process.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_d78d73_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_565f0a80bf",
        "created_at": 1794503096.5316408
      },
      "rev_n_4_a28466_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_a28466_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_a28466",
        "thought_text": "The current implementation of the Bachgold function is incorrect because it returns all prime factors of the given number instead of finding the maximum number of prime numbers that sum up to the given number.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4610483777028851476",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794503122.8950114
      },
      "rev_n_5_65fb47_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_65fb47_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_65fb47",
        "thought_text": "A more efficient approach would be to use a greedy algorithm that starts with the smallest prime number (2) and keeps subtracting it from the given number until it cannot be divided evenly anymore. Then, it moves on to the next smallest prime number and repeats the process until the given number is reduced to zero.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4610483777028851476",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794503122.8950264
      },
      "rev_n_6_d1575d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_d1575d_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_d1575d",
        "thought_text": "Another strategy could involve using dynamic programming to store intermediate results and avoid redundant calculations. This way, we can build up the solution incrementally, starting from smaller numbers and working our way up to the given number.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4610483777028851476",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794503122.8950346
      },
      "rev_n_5_65fb47_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_65fb47_1",
        "node_id": "n_5_65fb47",
        "thought_text": "Thought-2: To address the issue, we need to ensure that the input is read correctly and processed line by line. Since the input consists of a single integer, we should simply read this integer directly without attempting to read multiple lines. Additionally, we should focus on finding a way to represent the given number `n` as a sum of the maximum possible number of prime numbers efficiently. Instead of using a greedy algorithm that repeatedly subtracts the smallest prime number, we can consider a different approach that ensures we maximize the number of primes used. One effective method is to start with the largest possible prime numbers and work our way down, ensuring that each prime is used as many times as possible before moving to the next smaller prime. This approach helps in achieving the goal of maximizing the number of primes in the summation.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-4610483777028851476",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_5_65fb47_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_812f83b8ee",
        "created_at": 1794503137.3611612
      },
